{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 2480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physics is this close to understanding \nthe entire universe. And what lives in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this gap? Many physicists think it’s the \nelusive graviton—the quantum particle of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity—whose discovery will finally allow \nus to stitch together our two great theories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19040,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nature into a single master theory. But \nwhat is the graviton, and does it even exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91480,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum revolution started when we realized \nthat light is made of particles—photons. Max  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck guessed it and Albert Einstein proved \nit. Light is a wave in the electromagnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field, and so even from the beginning of \nmodern physics we have this idea that a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107920,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force—the electromagnetic force—is communicated \nby particles. As quantum mechanics evolved into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum field theory, we also found the particles \nfor the weak and the strong forces. That’s three  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the four fundamental forces, leaving only \ngravity lacking a mediating particle. So, if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we could just figure out the graviton, we’d have \nunified all the forces of nature and be on track  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a theory of everything. We’re now 100 years \nafter the birth of quantum mechanics, and much of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141120,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past century of work towards this master that \nhinges on the existence of the graviton. But does  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it exist? If not, much of that work in unifying \nour theories has been in the wrong direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can describe nearly the entire universe \nin terms of two theories—quantum mechanics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and general relativity. Yet they look \nwildly different to each other. taOn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum side we have these \nfields filling all of space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whose oscillations give us all known particles of \nmatter and three of the four fundamental forces.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174320,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These quantum fields fracture into discrete \nshards as you zoom in, and whose properties and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles can never be pinned down precisely. \nOn the relativity side we have space and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merging into a unified fabric of spacetime; a \nfabric that can expand and warp. It stretches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in response to matter and energy, leading to \nwhat we experience as the force of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a pretty effective picture. Quantum \ntheory gives us the stuff of the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity gives us the container. Matter in \nspacetime. Matter tells spacetime how to curve and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime tells matter how to move. So, if this \npicture works so well in describing the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why do we need to try to force gravity to \nwork the same way as the quantum forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, partly because the two theories are \nso different. We expect, or at least hope,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that all the complexity we observe in the universe \nemerges from unified underlying laws. That proved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be true of all matter and most of the forces \nof nature—quantum fields all the way down. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be weird if the most fundamental layer of \nreality was actually two unrelated things. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extra weird if the theories describing those \nthings are profoundly different—fundamentally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discrete and random for the quantum, fundamentally \ncontinuous and deterministic for relativity. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264080,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe that’s just how it is. Not very elegant, \nbut does the universe care what we find pretty?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s another reason besides \naesthetics that we need quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory and relativity theory to connect. \nThat’s because at a fundamental level,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they actively contradict each other. \nTogether they describe most of the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in certain places like the center \nof a black hole or at the big bang,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they generate contradictions and paradoxes \nthat, it seems, can only be solved by finding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the master theory in which quantum mechanics \nand general relativity connect seamlessly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For various reasons, most physicists trying \nto solve this problem follow a particular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307040,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "path. The path of particles. They try \nto quantize gravity, just as they once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantized electromagnetism. And the search \nfor this theory of quantum gravity implies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a particle that mediates gravity. That’s \nthe graviton. If the graviton exists,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then gravity has to be quantum, and vice \nversa. Let’s see why that’s the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get a photon, we start with the classical \nelectromagnetic field—the one described by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maxwell’s equations. A field in that sense \nis just something that extends through space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and has some value at each point in space. \nThat value can be a simple number. Like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349840,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the density or temperature of the air in the room,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giving a density or temperature field. Or it \ncan be a vector—a number and direction. The EM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field is like that—a strength of the force \nand a direction at every point in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fields can support waves—oscillations in \nfield strength that move through space. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372880,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density field in air supports sound waves. The EM \nfield supports EM waves, which we know as light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, when Planck and Einstein showed that light is \nmade up of indivisible particles, we were set on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388240,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the path to quantizing the EM field itself. This \nprocess of “quantization” followed the development  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum mechanics in the mid 20s and quantum \nfield theory in the following couple of decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Through quantization, the EM field is \nbroken down into countless modes of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all different frequencies. In this \nnew description, even the empty or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415120,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum-state of the EM field is made of those \nmodes—made of infinite electromagnetic waves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stacked and canceled into nothing. And those \n“virtual” modes are made of discrete packets,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428960,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as “real” EM waves—so the EM \nfield is made of virtual photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum field theory, we describe the quantum \nelectromagnetic force in terms of particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443120,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exchanging these virtual photons. They take \nmomentum from one particle and give it to another,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449760,
    "dDurationMs": 9280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causing the push and pull that we see as a smooth \nEM force. Photons mediate electromagnetism. We’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talked about whether these virtual photons can \nbe thought of as “real” before. But what IS real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the fact that the EM field is extremely well \ndescribed as being made up of quantized chunks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we use basically the exact same \nprocess to quantize slightly more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complicated fields we get the other quantum \nforces and their mediating particles—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gluons for the strong force and \nthe W and Z bosons for the weak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It should not be taken lightly that this stuff \nworks so incredibly well. We take a classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field, make some symmetry arguments to \nguess what other fields might exist,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apply quantization rules, and boom, we figure \nout almost all of the particles and forces that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make up our universe. The standard model \nof particle physics is arguably the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "successful theory in physics, and perhaps \nscience. It’s not surprising that we’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513920,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to envelop gravity and spacetime into the \nexplanatory magnificence of quantum field theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, how do we proceed? Well, we need \nto identify the field that we’re trying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to quantize and then apply the quantization \nrules just like we did with the other forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do know that gravity behaves like \nelectromagnetism in important ways. It has a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strength at all points in space. It also supports \nwaves—gravitational waves—that also travel at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speed of light. There are differences, \nhowever. According to general relativity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational field is not a field on \ntop of spacetime, like electromagnetism,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather in a very real sense it’s the fabric \nof spacetime itself. This tangling of the field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and its .. is going to be a problem as \nwe’ll see, but for now let’s proceed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s get a little more precise. \nIn the Einstein field equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field we’re interested in is encapsulated in \nsomething called the metric tensor—a 10-valued  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object made up of scalars and vectors with a \nvalue at every point in space. It’s a field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "albeit a very complicated one. Where the EM field \ncan be thought of as a thing lying on top of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588960,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "background grid of spacetime, this tensor field \nof GR in a real sense IS the grid of spacetime.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In electromagnetism, the field changes with \nrespect to the grid. In general relativity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601680,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the grid itself changes. This is going \nto prove complicated, but nonetheless,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this grid, this spacetime, this tensor \nfield is the thing we need to quantize."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s do that. We’ll start with an \napproach that hopefully won’t break anything.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619840,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to treat the gravitational field as a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small fluctuation – or perturbation – to \nan imaginary flat and static background.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We poke flat spacetime very lightly and \nsee what comes out. Hopefully a graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a very standard approach in physics. \nIt’s called perturbation theory. Rather than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641200,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "completely changing our model, we just make a \ntiny adjustment to what we understand and see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what happens. The result is a good approximation \nto reality, assuming the perturbation is very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654960,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small. Take for example electromagnetism—we \ncan understand its quantum behavior by taking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "account of the exchange of different nudges \nto the electromagnetic field as represented by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the exchange of virtual photons in different \nways. For a weak interaction, only the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obvious exchanges matter—like the exchange of \na single virtual photon. Stronger interactions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "require us to account for more complex \nexchanges, but it all works out beautifully."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we do this to the gravitational \nfield everything is … also fine!  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not only can we do a sort of gentle \nquantization of a weak gravitational field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we get a glimpse of the quantum of that \nfield that mediates the gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force—the graviton. What does it look like? \nWell, it inherits all of its properties in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pretty non-negotiable way from the \nfield it comes from. It’s a massless,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719280,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin-2 boson. Massless means it travels at \nthe speed of light. Spin 2 means it has a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726560,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "180 degree rotational symmetry that reflects \nthe sort of stretch-squish action on space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like the gravitational waves that should \nbe built from these gravitons. And boson means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can stack infinite gravitons together, to \nmake gravitational waves. And just as virtual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745840,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons make the quantum EM field, we can build \nthe fabric of spacetime out of virtual gravitons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cool cool. In a sense we just succeeded \nin quantizing gravity. This approach of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perturbative quantum gravity, and \nit actually works. We can use it to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766160,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recover classical gravity. We can \neven use it to make predictions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Stephen Hawking’s prediction of Hawking \nradiation—leaky black holes. So why don’t we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just stitch this quantum gravity to the rest of \nquantum field theory and say we have a theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of everything? Why not add the graviton to the \nstandard model library and move on with our lives?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so fast. We want our final \ntheory to explain the whole universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but our perturbative quantum gravity only \nworks in the case of very weak gravity. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t work for places with extreme spacetime \ncurvature, like the centers of black holes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also want to actually verify our \nquantum gravity theory with experiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and our perturbative theory works only in \nsituations where it’s essentially impossible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to differentiate it from regular Einsteinian \ngravity with a human-buildable experiment.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821520,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We really need the full theory. So, to finish \nthe job of quantizing gravity we have to see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we can generalize this to a theory that works \neverywhere, including where gravity is extreme."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As gravity gets stronger, our perturbations \nget bigger and the corrections we need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842320,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make to our classical theory \nget larger. More technically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846480,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need higher-order corrections, represented by \nmore complex interactions via virtual gravitons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember how we do this for the quantum \nelectromagnetic interactions. We calculate our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859280,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum EM force by summing over all the possible \nvirtual interactions that could have occurred.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For weak EM fields it’s enough to include only \nthe most probable such interactions, and that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means the simplest ones. Like, the exchange of \na virtual photon or two. For stronger EM fields,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more complex interactions become important. \nThat includes so-called self-energy terms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884240,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which result from the feedback \nbetween the different fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887840,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These higher-order terms involve loops that appear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the many Feynman diagrams that we sum \ntogether to compute a strong interaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a previous episode we talked about the \nconnection between these loops and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of the electron, and how they \nappear to make that mass infinite.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these sorts of infinites can’t be real \nor we wouldn’t have a universe. In regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum field theory we have a way to remove \nthem called renormalization. It lets us sort of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "divide them out by grounding them in measured \nvalues, for example by measuring the true  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928560,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of the electron. This is possible with the \nregular quantum fields because the perturbative  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936160,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion—the sum of Feynman diagrams—”only” \ngenerate a finite number of infinities. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still possible to absorb these into a finite \nnumber of additional terms in our equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if “absorbing infinities” sounds a \nbit hokey, it works incredibly well. By  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grounding our otherwise-infinite predictions in \na few finite measurements of the real universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum field theory is able to make stunningly \nprecise predictions of many, many things. Not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967200,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bad trade-off. Depicting the EM field as being \nmade of virtual photons is incredibly successful,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which forces us to take seriously this \npicture of a quantized field. At some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level we believe that the EM and other \nquantum forces are mediated by particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we can do for gravity what \nwe just did for electromagnetism  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then we can make the same argument \nfor the reality of the graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we’re ramping up the strength of our \nquantum gravity, increasing the complexity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our perturbative approximation, and hoping we \ncan renormalize any infinities to get a sensible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory. And this is where everything goes \ncatastrophically wrong. The problem is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitons interact gravitationally. They interact \nwith themselves. The photon, on the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does not interact electromagnetically—at \nleast not directly. It interacts with, say,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron field, which feeds \nback on the EM field. That extra  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "step limits the complexity of the \nphoton self-interaction and limits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the number of self-energy loops, \nmaking renormalization possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039360,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a graviton can directly spawn other virtual \ngravitons which can spawn more virtual gravitons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046480,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ad infinitum. Instead of a finite number of \nself-energy loops we get an infinite number. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An infinite number of terms in our \nperturbative approximation that can’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058960,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be renormalized—canceled out—with a finite number \nof real measurements. In cases of strong gravity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our quantum gravity theory becomes meaningless. \nWe say that perturbative quantum gravity is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072880,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-renormalizable. We can also think of this \nin terms of the strength of the gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077040,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coupling—the gravitational constant—getting \nstronger as the field getting stronger,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084320,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultimately sending the field strength to infinity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This straightforward approach at quantizing \ngravity leads to nonsense. So maybe it follows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we can’t build the gravitational field out \nof gravitons after all. Does this mean that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098160,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "graviton doesn’t exist? Not really. What it means \nis that this simple picture of quantum gravity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we get by applying the same quantization \nmethods as the other forces isn’t right. There  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be a theory of quantum gravity that just can’t \nbe got to by the simple perturbative approach."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119280,
    "dDurationMs": 10880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interestingly, it’s the graviton itself that \nmay lead us to the final answer. Even though  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard approach didn’t get us all the way, \nthe picture of the graviton we got from it is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost certainly right—if the graviton exists \nat all, it has to be a massless, spin-2 boson.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1143200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nothing else can generate the Einstein \nfield equations in the classical limit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1149200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s why, when we find a \ntheory that predicts a massless,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154240,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin-2 boson our ears prick up and we wonder \nif we’ve found a potential for quantum gravity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s happened once before, giving us \nstring theory. This was originally meant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be a theory for quark interactions, but \na graviton-like particle popped out of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "math in such a profound way that it sparked a \nmany-decade quest to find a stringy theory of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything. You can’t avoid the graviton in \nstring theory, and in string theory you also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "avoid the rampant infinities because. At the \nsmallest scales where gravity can be strongest,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the graviton field is smeared out over the \nvibrating strings at the theory’s core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other approaches to quantum gravity, \nlike loop quantum gravity and others. All  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of them have a graviton, because without the \ngraviton there’s no path to classical gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s again sounding like the graviton \nis inevitable. But again, no. It’s only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212960,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inevitable if gravity is fundamentally quantum \nin the first place. If the final theory looks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219200,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more like quantum mechanics than general \nrelativity. What’s the alternative? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s that the fabric of spacetime doesn’t have \ndiscrete chunks—it’s not made of gravitons. Maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1233360,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a smooth, continuous spacetime is fundamental in \nsome way. A number theories favour this option.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1240400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even Roger Penrose is a proponent, encouraging \nus to “gravitize the quantum” rather than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1246720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantizing gravity. Fit quantum mechanics into \ngeneral relativity rather than the way around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, finding evidence of the actual \nexistence of the graviton will be critical to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1259440,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confirming the quantum nature of gravity and even \nwhich quantum gravity we have. As I hinted, that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1267520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be really, really hard to do directly \nbecause we’d need to build solar-system-sized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1272640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle accelerators. Fortunately there are \nbrilliant methods to observe the indirect effects  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1278560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the interactions of a quantum graviton that we \ncan do now. For example, spotting the mediation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1285120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum entanglement by a gravitational field \nor a Cavendish experiment, but with exceptionally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1291440,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny masses. One of these methods may pan out very \nsoon, or we may have to start astroengineering our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1299120,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mega-collider. Nature has set a last gigantic \nhurdle to understanding her deepest laws. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1306640,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to understand and then find the graviton, and \nthen we’ll hold the building block of spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
